| **Factor**                      | **Ahmed (2024)** – *Applied Computational Intelligence & Soft Computing*                                     | **Boujmiraz et al. (2026)** – *Computers and Education: AI*                                        | **Ahmed, Wani, Hammad et al. (2025)** – *Scientific Reports*                                                                     |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Type of Study**               | Original research                                                                                            | Systematic literature review (PRISMA)                                                              | Original research                                                                                                                |
| **Dataset**                     | Real institutional data from Wollo University, Kombolcha Institute of Technology (2017–2022 student records) | 281 records screened → 72 studies retained (2017–2024) from ScienceDirect, IEEE Xplore, Scopus     | Two Kaggle datasets: (1) 10,000 rows / 6 features; (2) 6,607 rows / 20 features                                                  |
| **Methods / Algorithms Used**   | K-Means clustering (Davies-Bouldin) + KNN, SVM, Decision Trees, Naïve Bayes                                  | Reviews ML + DL methods used across studies; no new model proposed                                 | 9 regressors: Linear, Ridge, KNN, SVR, RF, Bagging, XGBoost, CatBoost, AdaBoost + proposed Ensemble Voting Regressor             |
| **Key Factors / Features**      | Gender, region, entrance result, number of previous attempts, studied credits, disability                    | Demographic, behavioral, psychological, and socioeconomic factors                                  | Study hours, previous scores, sleep hours, extracurriculars, papers practiced, attendance, parental involvement, teacher quality |
| **Best-Performing Model**       | **SVM** achieved the highest accuracy among compared classifiers                                             | N/A — systematic review; notes Decision Trees and rule-based methods as common strong predictors   | **Ensemble Voting Regressor** — R² = 0.989 (Dataset 1), R² = 0.77 (Dataset 2)                                                    |
| **Explainability (XAI)**        | Not addressed                                                                                                | Central theme; highlights limited adoption of XAI despite its importance for pedagogical decisions | Uses **SHAP + LIME**; Previous Scores identified as the most influential feature (~62% contribution)                             |
| **Evaluation Metrics**          | Accuracy comparison across classifiers                                                                       | N/A — qualitative/systematic review                                                                | MAE, RMSE, R², 10-fold cross-validation, paired t-tests                                                                          |
| **Gap / Limitation Identified** | Does not explore deep learning; small regional dataset limits generalizability                               | Limited studies connect ML/DL predictions with actual explainability and interpretability          | Feature selection sometimes reduced performance; deep learning not tested; socioeconomic/psychological features not included     |
| **Future Work Suggested**       | Expand dataset scope and try additional algorithms                                                           | Encourage wider adoption of XAI in educational predictive models                                   | Explore deep learning/transformers, add socioeconomic factors, and develop real-time adaptive learning systems                   |





| # | Paper Name                                                                                                                      | Year | Conference                                                                                         | Main Method / Technology                              | Main Contribution                                                               | Key Result / Finding                                                                | Limitations                                                             | Research Gap                                                                | How It Helps Our Project                                                                 |
| - | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1 | Student Success Prediction Using a Novel Machine Learning Approach Based on Modified SVM                                        | 2024 | ICMAT23                                                                                            | Modified SVM, Machine Learning, Classification        | Proposes an ML-based approach for predicting student success.                   | ML classification can effectively predict student success.                          | Performance depends on the dataset and selected features.               | Automated comparison and selection of suitable ML models is needed.         | Helps us compare classification algorithms automatically using AutoML.                   |
| 2 | Predicting Students’ Academic Grade Using Machine Learning Algorithms with Hybrid Feature Selection Approach                    | 2022 | ICACC 2022                                                                                         | Machine Learning, Hybrid Feature Selection            | Combines feature selection with ML algorithms for academic grade prediction.    | Relevant feature selection can improve prediction performance.                      | Results may depend on the dataset and selected features.                | Automated feature selection and model optimization can be explored.         | Helps us automate feature selection and model selection.                                 |
| 3 | Predicting Students’ Final Exam Scores Based on Their Regularity of Engagement with Pre-Class Activities in a Flipped Classroom | 2024 | CSEDU 2024                                                                                         | Learning Analytics, Regression, Machine Learning      | Uses student engagement data to predict final examination scores.               | Student engagement can provide useful information for predicting exam performance.  | Results may depend on the specific course and learning environment.     | More diverse features and automated model optimization are needed.          | Helps us identify engagement and learning activity as useful prediction features.        |
| 4 | Impact of English Proficiency on Academic Performance of Software Engineering Students                                          | 2021 | 4th International Conference on Data Storage and Data Engineering                                  | Statistical Analysis, Correlation Analysis            | Examines the relationship between English proficiency and academic performance. | English proficiency shows a relationship with academic performance.                 | Focuses mainly on one factor and a specific student population.         | Multiple academic, behavioral and demographic factors should be considered. | Helps us identify additional student attributes that can be used as prediction features. |
| 5 | Study Habits and Academic Performance: A Comparative Analysis                                                                   | 2021 | Conference Publication                                                                             | Statistical Analysis, Comparative Analysis            | Studies the relationship between study habits and academic performance.         | Study habits can be associated with students’ academic performance.                 | Limited variables and student population.                               | Larger datasets and ML-based prediction using multiple features are needed. | Helps us consider study habits as a predictive feature.                                  |
| 6 | Optimizing Hybrid Learning Outcomes via Clustering-Guided Data Mining Techniques in Higher Education                            | 2025 | 3rd International Conference on Communication, Security, and Artificial Intelligence (ICCSAI 2025) | K-Means, DBSCAN, Hierarchical Clustering, Data Mining | Uses clustering to identify different student learning profiles.                | Clustering can identify groups of students with different learning characteristics. | Mainly focuses on clustering rather than direct performance prediction. | Clustering can be combined with prediction and automated model selection.   | Helps us explore student grouping before applying performance prediction.                |

